<Base title="Why Kota" description="Kota already has the physics and maths talent, the teachers and the city that a quantum workforce needs. The case, with sources, for making Kota India's next quantum city.">
  <section class="article why">
    <div class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why Kota</p>
      <h1>Quantum needs physics talent. Kota already has it.</h1>
      <p class="desc">Every year Kota trains lakhs of India's most determined physics, chemistry and maths students. Then almost all of them leave. Kota Quantum Valley asks a simple question: what if the next industry that needs exactly those skills grew here instead?</p>

      <dl class="stats why-stats">
        <div><dt>aspirants at the peak</dt><dd>~2.5 lakh</dd></div>
        <div><dt>hostels</dt><dd>~4,000</dd></div>
        <div><dt>paying-guest homes</dt><dd>~45,000</dd></div>
        <div><dt>coaching institutes</dt><dd>~35</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="meta">Figures reported by PTI in March 2026 [1].</p>

      <h2>What Kota already has</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card"><h3>Talent</h3><p>Students who spend years solving hard physics and maths problems every day. That is the foundation of quantum science.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Teachers</h3><p>A city full of people whose profession is explaining difficult science clearly. Quantum skilling needs exactly that.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>A ready city</h3><p>Thousands of hostels and paying-guest homes, built for a young population, at a lower cost than the big metros.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Institutions</h3><p>IIIT Kota, an Institute of National Importance with a permanent campus at Ranpur [5], and Rajasthan Technical University.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>Why the quantum industry needs what Kota has</h2>
      <p>A quantum computer is not built by physicists alone. It needs people who understand quantum mechanics and linear algebra, but also electronics engineers who build the control hardware, firmware and software engineers who make it run, and teachers who can train thousands more. Course 1 explains this in its lesson on <a href="/posts/superconducting-qubits-and-control-electronics/">qubits and control electronics</a>.</p>
      <p>Kota's students already have the hardest part, the physics and the mathematics, at an age when most people are only starting it. What they lack is a reason to stay and a path from JEE preparation to quantum work. That path is what this site is building, one course at a time.</p>

      <h2>Kota needs a second engine</h2>
      <p>Kota's economy depends on one industry. According to PTI, coaching enrolments fell by about 30 to 40 per cent between 2023 and 2025, to roughly 85,000 to 1 lakh students, and the industry's annual revenue fell from about ₹6,500–7,000 crore to about ₹3,500 crore. Early figures for the 2026–27 session show a 20 to 30 per cent recovery [1]. A city that depends on one industry is fragile. Deep-technology training and research could use the same hostels, teachers and students, and give graduates a reason to come back.</p>

      <h2>Why now</h2>
      <div class="why-now">
        <div class="card"><h3>India</h3><p>The National Quantum Mission was approved with about ₹6,000 crore for 2023–24 to 2030–31 [2]. Four thematic hubs started in 2024–25, at IISc Bengaluru (computing), IIT Madras with C-DOT (communication), IIT Bombay (sensing) and IIT Delhi (materials and devices), and by February 2026 eight startups had been supported [3].</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Andhra Pradesh</h3><p>The Amaravati Quantum Valley is coming up on a 50-acre campus with IBM, TCS and L&amp;T as key partners [6, 7]. In July 2026 IBM said Amaravati will host one of the first two IBM quantum computers in India [8].</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>Rajasthan</h3><p>In June 2026 the Union government announced an advanced quantum computing and quantum communications lab at MNIT Jaipur [4], which also mentors IIIT Kota [5]. The state approved its Semiconductor Policy in March 2026 [9]. We could not find a state quantum mission yet. That is the gap.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>What Kota needs</h2>
      <ol class="why-list">
        <li><strong>A Rajasthan quantum mission</strong> that names Kota as a hub, as Andhra Pradesh has done for Amaravati.</li>
        <li><strong>A quantum lab at IIIT Kota or RTU</strong>, open to students, connected to the new lab at MNIT Jaipur.</li>
        <li><strong>Quantum skilling programmes built with Kota's coaching ecosystem</strong>: bridge courses that take JEE-level physics and maths into quantum computing.</li>
        <li><strong>Internships with the national hubs and industry</strong>, so that students can work on real quantum hardware and software.</li>
        <li><strong>An incubator for deep-tech startups</strong> that want to stay in Kota.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What we are doing about it</h2>
      <p>We start with what anyone can do today: free, in-depth teaching. <a href="/courses/">Four courses</a>, quantum basics, mathematics, programming in C and Python, and the IBM Quantum series, take a Class 10 student all the way to real quantum work, and a <a href="/lab/">Quantum Lab</a> lets them experiment in the browser. Read <a href="/about/">who is behind this</a>.</p>

